# DEFECT 060, found 2026-09-17 by panel 161's ffi-pragmatist beside the `char`
# question, reproduced and repaired 2026-09-18.
#
# SYMPTOM: a header struct with any `const` member could not be built from
# Heroes, and the compiler said so in its own voice: `internal error: compiling
# the generated C failed`, exit 2, followed by clang's raw wording about a
# variable `t3` the author never wrote. That is the compiler taking the blame
# for an `extern` in a `.hero` file — three of the four things design.md §4.17
# exists to prevent, in one message, and the exact shape CLAUDE.md §7's named
# exception is for.
#
# CAUSE: every Heroes value is built by ASSIGNMENT — the emitter writes
# `Locked t3; t3 = (Locked){…};` — and C11 6.5.16.1 forbids assigning to a
# struct with a const-qualified member. clang says
# `cannot assign to variable 't3' with const-qualified data member 'id'`, on a
# `#line` that already points at the author's own file. So the LOCATION was
# right all along and only the verdict was wrong: `emit/ffi.hero`'s `explain()`
# walks clang's stderr through twenty readers and not one of them recognised
# this wording, so it returned nothing, `blamed()` answered no, and the failure
# fell through to exit 2.
#
# FIX: a twenty-first reader, `emit/ffi_field.const_member`. It recovers the
# member name from clang's error line and asks which of THIS program's group
# records declares a field of that name — §7's `declaration()` narrowing
# unchanged, so a `const` member in anybody else's struct still stays exit 2 and
# the compiler's fault. It reads the ERROR line alone and never the warnings
# above it, because clang names the struct's type only there and holding state
# across clang's output is a premise about clang's layout that panel 038 already
# paid for once.
#
# WHY NO SITTING: the repair does not move the line between accepted and
# refused. This program was refused before and is refused after; what changed is
# who is blamed and what the message says. A new member of an existing class
# whose own narrowing rule admits it, and the judgement is written here so a
# later reader can disagree with it.
#
# THE SHAPES BESIDE IT, and two are still unrun (CL-061): a `const` member in a
# `partial` record, a nested record holding one, and a `const` ARRAY member —
# panel 161's ffi-pragmatist measured that `const char[N]` is refused at every
# spelling and that it is the `const` and not the `char`, since
# `const int32_t n[4]` is refused too. Whether those carry a good message or
# this same internal error is not measured here.

extern "ffi-const-member.h"
    record Locked
        id: i32  #~ ffi_const_field
        n: i32
    function locked_id(l: Locked) -> i32

function main()
    # Reading a `Locked` the library hands back is sound and is not what this
    # case is about; BUILDING one is what C refuses.
    x = Locked(id: 7, n: 1)
    print(x.locked_id().to_i64().must())
